course: dictionary
lesson: 1897
title: Who were the colonisers?
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A coloniser is a country or person who takes control of another land, settles there and rules the people who already live there. Colonisers came from far away, often across the sea, and wanted the land's riches and power.
explain: Read «Imagine new neighbours moving into your street and announcing that your house is "unclaimed" because they had not seen it before.» The neighbours do not own the house, and it is not empty just because they have not seen it. The quotation marks around unclaimed are a clue: the writer shows that colonisers wrongly pretended the land belonged to nobody.
explain: Now read «European colonisers forced Indigenous people to mine silver in Peru and Bolivia, and enslaved Africans to dig gold in Brazil.» The colonisers are the ones giving the orders and making people work for them. Ask yourself who has the power in each sentence. That tells you who the coloniser is.
fact: Coloniser means a country or person that takes control of another land, settles there and rules the people who already live there.
fact: The colonisers are the ones doing the taking; the colonised are the people whose land is taken.
fact: «European colonisers» means colonisers who came from countries in Europe, such as Britain, France, Spain and Portugal.
fact: «Indigenous» means the first people who lived in a place, long before newcomers arrived.
fact: In American spelling it is written colonizer, with a z, but the meaning is the same.
exercise: What does coloniser mean? | A person who grows and sells colourful flowers | *A country or person that takes control of another land and rules its people | A person who writes history books | A kind of tool for digging gold
exercise: In «European colonisers forced Indigenous people to mine silver in Peru», who was giving the orders? | The Indigenous people who were forced to work | The silver miners' children | *The European colonisers | The horses that carried the silver
exercise: Which word in «your house is "unclaimed"» shows that the newcomers wrongly thought nobody owned it? | moving | street | *unclaimed | neighbours
test: What is the difference between a coloniser and a colonised person? | They are two names for the same people | The colonised person is the one who rules the land | *The coloniser takes control of the land, and the colonised people are the ones who already lived there and lost control | Colonised means very old
test: Which place is NOT named in the example sentence about silver and gold? | Brazil | Peru | Bolivia | *Egypt
exam: Why is unclaimed in quotation marks in the example about the new neighbours? | To show the house was made of gold | To show the neighbours were very quiet | *To show that the colonisers wrongly believed the land belonged to nobody | To show the word is a sport
